[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"PL/SQL Cursors (Implicit and Explicit) in Oracle DB"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cursors are essential for processing data retrieved by SQL statements in PL/SQL. They act as pointers to a private SQL area, enabling you to fetch and manipulate data row by row. Oracle provides two types of cursors: implicit and explicit."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Implicit Cursors"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Implicit cursors are automatically created and managed by Oracle for all DML (Data Manipulation Language) statements (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) and single-row SELECT statements. You don't need to declare or open them explicitly. However, you can access information about the most recently executed implicit cursor using cursor attributes."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Automatically managed by Oracle."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Used for DML statements and single-row SELECT statements."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Accessed via cursor attributes (e.g., SQL%ROWCOUNT, SQL%FOUND, SQL%NOTFOUND, SQL%ISOPEN)."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"DECLARE
  v_emp_id NUMBER := 100;
BEGIN
  UPDATE employees
  SET salary = salary * 1.10
  WHERE employee_id = v_emp_id;

  IF SQL%FOUND THEN
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Employee updated successfully.');
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Number of rows affected: ' || SQL%ROWCOUNT);
  ELSE
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Employee not found.');
  END IF;

  COMMIT;
EXCEPTION
  WHEN OTHERS THEN
    ROLLBACK;
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('An error occurred: ' || SQLERRM);
END;
/"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, SQL%FOUND checks if the UPDATE statement affected any rows, and SQL%ROWCOUNT returns the number of rows updated. SQL%ISOPEN is always FALSE for implicit cursors because Oracle automatically closes them."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Explicit Cursors"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explicit cursors are used for multi-row SELECT statements, giving you more control over data retrieval. You must explicitly declare, open, fetch from, and close them."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Manually managed by the developer."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Used for multi-row SELECT statements."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Requires declaration, opening, fetching, and closing."},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The basic steps for using an explicit cursor are:"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Declare the cursor: Define the cursor with a SELECT statement."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Open the cursor: Allocate memory and prepare the cursor for fetching data."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fetch data: Retrieve data from the cursor into variables."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Close the cursor: Release the memory allocated to the cursor."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"DECLARE
  CURSOR emp_cursor IS
    SELECT employee_id, first_name, last_name
    FROM employees
    WHERE department_id = 80;

  v_emp_id employees.employee_id%TYPE;
  v_first_name employees.first_name%TYPE;
  v_last_name employees.last_name%TYPE;

BEGIN
  OPEN emp_cursor;

  LOOP
    FETCH emp_cursor INTO v_emp_id, v_first_name, v_last_name;
    EXIT WHEN emp_cursor%NOTFOUND;

    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Employee ID: ' || v_emp_id || ', Name: ' || v_first_name || ' ' || v_last_name);
  END LOOP;

  CLOSE emp_cursor;
EXCEPTION
  WHEN OTHERS THEN
    IF emp_cursor%ISOPEN THEN
      CLOSE emp_cursor;
    END IF;
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('An error occurred: ' || SQLERRM);
END;
/"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `emp_cursor` is declared to select employee data from department 80. The cursor is opened, and a loop fetches data row by row until `emp_cursor%NOTFOUND` becomes TRUE, indicating that all rows have been processed. Finally, the cursor is closed to release resources. The exception handler ensures the cursor is closed even if an error occurs."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Cursor Attributes"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Both implicit and explicit cursors have attributes that provide information about their state and the results of the last executed SQL statement. These attributes are accessed using the syntax `cursor_name%attribute_name`."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"%FOUND: Returns TRUE if the last fetch returned a row; otherwise, returns FALSE."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"%NOTFOUND: Returns TRUE if the last fetch did not return a row; otherwise, returns FALSE. It's the logical opposite of %FOUND."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"%ISOPEN: Returns TRUE if the cursor is currently open; otherwise, returns FALSE."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"%ROWCOUNT: Returns the number of rows affected by the last SQL statement executed through the cursor."},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always close explicit cursors after use to release resources. Failing to do so can lead to performance issues and resource exhaustion."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use cursor FOR loops for simpler cursor handling. Oracle implicitly opens, fetches from, and closes the cursor in a cursor FOR loop."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Handle exceptions properly to ensure cursors are closed even if errors occur."},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"DECLARE
  CURSOR emp_cursor IS
    SELECT employee_id, first_name, last_name
    FROM employees
    WHERE department_id = 80;
BEGIN
  FOR emp_rec IN emp_cursor LOOP
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Employee ID: ' || emp_rec.employee_id || ', Name: ' || emp_rec.first_name || ' ' || emp_rec.last_name);
  END LOOP;
EXCEPTION
  WHEN OTHERS THEN
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('An error occurred: ' || SQLERRM);
END;
/"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates a cursor FOR loop, which simplifies the cursor handling process. Oracle automatically opens the cursor, fetches data into the `emp_rec` record, and closes the cursor when all rows have been processed or an exception occurs."}]
